  ],
  "text": "Your phone holds somewhere around fifteen billion transistors. Every single one is a switch. Before them, a switch with no moving parts meant a glass tube the size of your thumb. A room sized computer held a few thousand of those, and they burned out constantly. A transistor does the same job in solid material, with nothing to wear out. And once a switch is that cheap, you stop counting them altogether. Wire a few together and you get a gate that answers one yes or no question. Wire gates together and you get addition. Everything else is built on that. For decades the count doubled about every two years, and the features kept shrinking. Now the smallest features are a handful of nanometres. Tens of atoms across. Which is why the doubling is slowing. You cannot build a switch out of half an atom. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
